{# THE PAGE A FRESH INSTALLATION HAS BEFORE IT HAS ANYTHING ELSE.

   A plant is the seam and the shell and nothing else: no modules, no routes, no
   controllers, so nothing answers `/` and Symfony shows its welcome-404. That
   is a correct installation looking like a broken one, and the first thing a
   new operator sees.

   The shell cannot fix it with a route — it owns no URLs, and the boundary test
   fails the build the day it does. It can ship the PAGE. The application points
   a URL at it in its own config/routes/shell.yaml, using Symfony's
   TemplateController, and owns both the URL and the decision to replace this
   with a real home screen later.

   THIS TEMPLATE NAMES NO MODULE, and the boundary sweep reads it like every
   other file here. It names two PACKAGES — the seam and the shell — because
   those are the two that are actually installed, and "the seam" is a word that
   means nothing to somebody on their first day.

   It types no furniture: it fills sockets, like any module page should. #}
{% extends '@UhifadhiShell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Welcome{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_title %}A fresh uhifadhi installation{% endblock %}
{% block shell_page_subtitle %}
    Two packages are installed and nothing else. This page says what they are,
    why the sidebar beside it is empty, and what to do next.
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page %}
    <div class="welcome">
        <h2 class="zone">What is installed</h2>

        <div class="grid g2">
            <div class="c wcard">
                <div class="wcard-head">
                    <span class="mono wpkg">uhifadhi/seam-module</span>
                    <span class="chip acc">the seam</span>
                </div>
                <p>Where every module registers. It keeps the catalogue of what
                   this installation can do, the areas that work is organised
                   by, and the permissions that decide who sees which of it.
                   Nothing draws anything here — it is the ledger the rest of
                   the platform reads.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="c wcard">
                <div class="wcard-head">
                    <span class="mono wpkg">uhifadhi/shell-module</span>
                    <span class="chip acc">the shell</span>
                </div>
                <p>The frame around this very page: the sidebar on the left, the
                   bar across the top, the page furniture holding this text, and
                   the theme all three are drawn in. It remembers nothing and
                   knows no module by name; it draws what it is handed.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="wlead">Between them they are the whole sentence the platform
           is built on: <b>a module registers with the seam and renders in the
           shell.</b> Everything a deployment can actually do arrives as a
           module on top of these two.</p>

        <h2 class="zone">Why the sidebar is empty</h2>

        <p>Because no modules are installed yet. The sidebar has no rows and the
           module grid has no cards for the same reason, and neither is a
           placeholder waiting to be filled in by hand: both are drawn live from
           what is installed, so an installation with nothing in it honestly
           shows nothing. Zero of everything is a working installation, not a
           half-finished one.</p>

        <h2 class="zone">What to do next</h2>

        <p>Install a module. Each one is an ordinary Composer package named
           <code class="mono">uhifadhi/&lt;name&gt;-module</code>:</p>

        <pre class="wpre mono">composer require uhifadhi/&lt;name&gt;-module</pre>

        <p>Symfony Flex registers the bundle and writes its configuration, the
           module registers itself with the seam, and its rows appear in the
           sidebar the next time you load a page. There is no list to edit and
           no menu to wire up. Removing the package takes its rows away again
           the same day.</p>

        <h2 class="zone">About this page</h2>

        <p>It is not part of the shell's furniture and it is not a route the
           shell owns — the shell ships no routes at all. This page is at
           <code class="mono">/</code> because your application's
           <code class="mono">config/routes/shell.yaml</code> points that URL at
           the template, and it is meant to be replaced: the day this
           installation grows a real home screen, change that file to name it
           and this page stops being reachable.</p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
